WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:29.910
MILLENNIAL WEALTH:
We’ve all heard it.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.530
The idea that Millennials are worse off than
any generation before them.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.860
59% of Millennials can’t afford a $1000
emergency expense.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.960
Most millennials can’t afford a home and
many are living with their parents.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:45.300
Nearly 75% of millennials are living paycheck
to paycheck yet 70% of them plan to leave

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.860
their jobs, and on and on and on.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:54.230
This widespread belief paints millennials
as all being broke, lazy, and downright financially

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:55.230
illiterate.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:59.770
And that all of this is thanks to the boomers
in government and the 2008 financial crisis

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.590
that permanently destroyed the economy for
younger generations.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:09.280
This is the mainstream narrative but it’s
also complete and utter boloney.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.850
Every single statistic that you see on those
headlines are cherry picked to push a narrative.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:17.880
Consider, for example, the statistic that
when boomers were in their early 30s, they

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.710
controlled 20% of the national wealth but
when millennials were in their early 30s,

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:26.400
they only controlled 3.5% of the national
wealth.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.290
This seems like a massive discrepancy and
it is but what most of these studies fail

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:36.570
to mention is the other discrepancies that
easily explain this like demographics.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:41.060
When boomers were growing up, the US population
was over a 100 million less.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.180
As such, boomers made up for a lot more of
the population than millennials did.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:50.649
In fact, when you account for this one discrepancy,
the wealth discrepancy not only goes away

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:52.610
but it actually inverts.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:54.350
I kid you not.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:59.560
Millennials, on a per capita basis, are actually
slightly wealthier than boomers were at the

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:00.700
same age.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.570
But how could that be?

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:05.610
Aren’t all millennials broke bums living
with their parents?

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:10.929
Well, the truth is that there's a whole nother
group of millennials that are never mentioned

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:13.980
that are richer than any generation before
them.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.959
And I’m not talking about some sort of elite
top 0.1%.

00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:23.959
I’m talking about the 35% who have net worths
over a $100,000, the 18% who have net worths

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:29.489
over a $250,000, the 9% who have net worths
over $500,000, and the 3% who are already

00:02:29.489 --> 00:02:31.040
millionaires.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.390
Keep in mind, millennials are predominantly
in their 30s right now.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:39.990
So, it’s safe to say that a large portion
of this 35% will end up as millionaires, multi

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.500
millionaires, and deca millionaires.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:46.879
Now of course, being a millionaire is already
not the same and it wont be the same in 30

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:52.780
years but it’s also a far cry from the narrative
that portrays Millennials solely as financially

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:56.320
struggling losers that are still reliant on
their parents.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.920
The reality is that a large portion of millennials
are actually way richer than you think.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:05.930
So, join me as we take a look at how this
narrative came to be and discover the truth

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.959
about millennial wealth.

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:17.010
IMPOSTER SYNDROME:
Alright, so if millennials are actually doing

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:21.840
just as well as previous generations on a
per captia basis, why do they get such a bad

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:22.840
rep?

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.390
Is it just a vocal minority or a media bias
or is their more to it?

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:31.849
Well, I think we can get a better idea when
we take a look at the wealthiest people from

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:33.250
each generation.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:39.780
The average net worth of millennials who consider
themselves wealthy is $531,000.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.549
On average, millennials needed $100,000 more
than Gen Xers to feel wealthy.

00:03:44.549 --> 00:03:49.290
The only generation that needed more money
than millennials to feel wealthy was Boomers

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.720
who on average needed $692,000.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.379
But wait a minute, that means that Millennials
have lower expectations than Boomers but aren’t

00:03:58.379 --> 00:04:02.970
even able to achieve these lower expectations
because of the crony economy right?

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:07.280
Well actually no because you have to consider
that they’re in completely different stages

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.450
of life.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:12.640
Boomers are predominantly in their 60s meaning
that they’re context is retirement.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.200
This means that Boomers on average think that
$692,000 is enough for retirement as a whole.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:23.830
Meanwhile, millennials are wanting $531,000
as 30 year olds with 30 more years in the

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:24.990
workforce.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.630
And taking a look at their retirement expectations,
this all makes sense.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:35.039
On average, millennials are wanting $1.8 million
for retirement or nearly triple what Boomers

00:04:35.039 --> 00:04:36.440
are looking for.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.780
Even high inflation and rising living costs
don’t account for that massive disparity

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:44.509
in expectations but you know what does?

00:04:44.509 --> 00:04:45.789
Perception.

00:04:45.789 --> 00:04:47.910
Wealth is a rather subjective topic.

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:52.950
$2.5 million might be middle class in San
Francisco but in Nebraska, that would be quite

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:53.950
wealthy.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:58.050
A significant portion of the perception that
Millennials are financially challenged stems

00:04:58.050 --> 00:04:59.980
from this subjectivity.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:04.880
In general, Millennials who are objectively
well-off may not consider themselves as such,

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.639
reinforcing the notion of Millennials' financial
struggles.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:12.650
This notion is less about their actual financial
well-being and more about their ambitious

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:13.770
aspirations.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.550
Again, let me reiterate.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:20.979
Millennials don’t even think half of a million
is enough to be considered wealthy while boomers

00:05:20.979 --> 00:05:24.910
think that $700,000 is wealthy enough to straight
up retire.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:30.419
But how does such a massive discrepancy in
wealth expectations manifest between generations.

00:05:30.419 --> 00:05:34.410
Well, the answer is easy: social media.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:38.350
Various studies have found that millennials
approach wealth from a comparative basis while

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:41.730
boomers tend to approach wealth from an objective
basis.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:46.949
For instance, a Boomer might evaluate their
financial status based on expenses: groceries,

00:05:46.949 --> 00:05:49.669
taxes, insurance premiums, and so on.

00:05:49.669 --> 00:05:54.470
If they can comfortably cover these expenses
for a prolonged period without active income,

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:57.050
they tend to classify themselves as wealthy.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:01.780
Millennials, on the other hand, don’t define
wealth based on sustainability.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:05.560
In fact, sustainability has nothing do with
their equation.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:10.419
Rather, they define wealth based on what their
richest friends can do.

00:06:10.419 --> 00:06:14.700
Being able to go on the same vacations, live
in the same luxury buildings, eat out at the

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.300
same restaurants, and so on.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:21.460
In fact, 47% of millennials compare their
lives to their peers on social media while

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:25.580
only 13% of boomers compare their lives to
their social circles.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:30.580
This explains why millennials have such a
bad financial rep despite doing just as well

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:32.460
as previous generations.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:38.531
The reality is that 50% of millennials are
actually just broke while the other 50% are

00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:43.220
actually pretty well off but they would never
describe themselves as such because their

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:46.860
expectations are multiple times higher than
any previous generation.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:52.150
Thus, the entire generation is frequently
misrepresented as financially disadvantaged

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.450
which isn’t true.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:57.720
But something that is true about millennials
is the raging wealth divide.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:06.190
WEALTH DIVIDE:
The 

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:11.840
media often frames the wealth divide as a
conflict between the top 1% and the rest of

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:12.840
society.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:18.440
However, the reality is more nuanced: it's
a struggle between the upper 50% and the lower

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:23.100
50, and I think this graph really showcases
this phenomenon.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:29.900
The top 50% have net worths anywhere between
$46,000 and $3 million which again is pretty

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:30.900
good.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:34.940
If we look at the left side of this graph
though, it’s gets ugly real quickly.

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:41.160
A net worth of $0 almost puts you in the 20th
percentile meaning that nearly 1 in 5 millennials

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.020
have a negative net worth.

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:49.039
So, the middle class is indeed disappearing
but not in the way that is often portrayed.

00:07:49.039 --> 00:07:50.820
Not everyone is becoming broke.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:55.530
The top half and especially the top quarter
are thriving in the new economy while the

00:07:55.530 --> 00:08:00.270
bottom half and especially the bottom quarter
are getting annihilated.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:01.650
But how did we get here?

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:07.349
Well, people often point the finger at politics
and capitalism, but I think the answer has

00:08:07.349 --> 00:08:12.720
more to do with 3 social trends starting with
the 2008 financial crisis.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.240
Discussions surrounding the recession typically
revolve around its impact on the job market,

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:19.479
economy, and opportunities.

00:08:19.479 --> 00:08:24.600
But the most important item that the recession
destroyed, atleast for millennials, was the

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:25.600
investing spirit.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:32.070
It’s kind of a meme that millennials are
the degenerates gambling on options and AMC

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:38.300
and NFTs and all of that crap, but that’s
actually such a small portion of millennials.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:42.599
The vast majority of millennials are actually
extremely conservative when it comes to investments.

00:08:42.599 --> 00:08:46.450
In fact, they prefer to just hoard cash.

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:52.310
65% of millennial wealth is held in cash,
the highest of any generation, and much of

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:54.290
the rest is in real estate.

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:59.730
Only a mere 15% is actually invested in stocks
which is really bad when you consider that

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:06.560
boomers have 24% invested stocks even though
they’re supposed to be risk averse ones.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:12.149
The 2008 financial crisis gave millennials
investing PTSD like no other generation.

00:09:12.149 --> 00:09:17.690
In fact, 57% of millennials admit that the
crisis still influences their investing choices

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:23.810
while the same is only true with 39% of Gen
Xers and just 13% of boomers.

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:28.470
Given that investing is the key to not just
retirement but financial freedom in general

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:33.350
especially in times of high inflation, it’s
no wonder why there’s such a wealth divide

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:34.570
amongst millennials.

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:38.850
There’s the half that invests and the half
that doesn’t.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:43.050
Many millennials would argue that they just
don’t have enough money to invest and this

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:47.250
is often true as well because many of them
are stuck in mountains of college debt.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:51.870
Of course, the skyrocketing price of tuition
very much plays a role here, but I believe

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:56.440
the crisis is more closely linked to the relevance
of education.

00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:00.540
Millennials were strongly urged to pursue
higher education, which was often touted as

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:03.610
the key to securing good jobs and success.

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:10.360
As such, 38% of millennials ended up getting
a degree while only 32% of gen Xers and 15%

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:11.930
of boomers have a degree.

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:17.130
However, this largely backfired as a lot of
millennials ended up getting degrees just

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:21.120
because they were told to do so resulting
in a bunch of expensive degrees that have

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.230
no value in the job market.

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:27.190
As such, many of these millennials would end
up getting jobs that didn’t even need a

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:31.830
degree or paid more for a degree making it
even harder to pay off the debt.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:35.550
If they didn’t end up in one of these jobs,
it’s more than likely that they ended up

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:39.269
in one of the new age gig economy jobs which
is even worse.

00:10:39.269 --> 00:10:43.240
We’re talking food delivery, ridesharing,
freelancers, etc.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:48.310
On paper, these jobs actually have pretty
good salaries but what they don’t have is

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:50.980
any benefits which makes them far worse.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:56.380
A police officer or teacher may only make
$60k, but they get another $60k worth of benefits

00:10:56.380 --> 00:11:00.930
in the form of health insurance, retirement
benefits, paid time off, set work hours, a

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:02.470
better tax situation, etc.

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:07.990
An uber driver making $60k on the other hand
is closer to earning minimum wage.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:12.730
So, I think much of the millennial wealth
divide can be explained by an aversion to

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:18.089
investing, irrelevant degrees associated with
massive college debt, and the modern gig economy.

00:11:18.089 --> 00:11:24.240
But, honestly, I think one factor trumps all
of these.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:30.930
TECH WEALTH:
I think almost the entirety of the millennial

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:36.350
wealth divide can be explained by one factor
and one factor alone: technology.

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:41.000
Millennials who have embraced tech are experiencing
remarkable success, while those who have not

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:42.990
find themselves struggling.

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:46.100
And I’m not just talking about coders and
project managers.

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:50.500
I’m talking about people who leveraged tech
vs people who did not.

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:52.550
You can see this in any profession.

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:57.350
The wealthiest lawyers are the one’s serving
tech companies, not the local judge.

00:11:57.350 --> 00:12:01.680
The wealthiest teachers are the ones selling
courses online, not the ones in public schools

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:03.970
or even Harvard professors.

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:08.860
The wealthiest accountants are the ones working
at tech companies, not the ones working at

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:10.470
the IRS.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:15.250
The wealthiest doctors are the ones on social
media, not even the ones doing neuro surgery.

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:19.769
While this inclination was already taking
shape during the boomer and Gen X generations,

00:12:19.769 --> 00:12:23.899
it has escalated to an unprecedented degree
amongst millennials.

00:12:23.899 --> 00:12:29.100
Before 2000, non tech people, in inflation
adjusted terms were making $40-60,000 while

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.620
tech people were making $70-90,000.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:35.240
Some might cross $100,000 but that was pretty
much it.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:40.440
Today, it’s still hard for non tech people
to reach $70 or $90,000 but tech people are

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:45.800
able to reach $200, $500, $1 million per year
by the time they’re 30 or 40.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:51.940
The disparity between non tech and tech people
is no longer 40% or 50%, it is straight up

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:53.810
a magnitude.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:58.800
And it’s no wonder why, the leverage, scale,
and efficiency that’s possible with tech

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:01.440
is incomparable to any traditional job.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:04.230
As such, their earnings potential is far higher.

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:07.430
Also, remember all of those shortfalls that
we were previously discussing?

00:13:07.430 --> 00:13:10.740
Well, those are automatically solved by tech.

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:12.550
Take stock investing for example.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:16.440
If you’re in tech, you’re either going
to be forced to take stock compensation whether

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:20.660
you like it or you’re earning so much that
even if you’re conservative with your stock

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:24.090
investing, it adds up to a substantial investment.

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:29.110
Also, if your degree aligns with the tech
sector, the ROI of college becomes astronomical

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:32.040
and paying back the debt becomes no problem
at all.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:36.790
In the realm of the gig economy, while certain
gigs like being an Uber driver might be less

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:41.610
appealing, other tech-driven opportunities
such as becoming a YouTuber, entering e-commerce,

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:46.540
owning Airbnb rentals, or being a remote tech
worker is extraordinarily favorable.

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:51.180
The bottomline is that the modern economy
works for tech people and people who are 1

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:53.430
to 2 degrees of separation away from tech.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:55.800
For everyone else, it sucks.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:59.810
And this is preciesly what’s manifesting
in millennial wealth.

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:04.920
50% are doing way better than you think but
the other 50% are doing way worse than you

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:08.930
think and that is the true state of Millennials.

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:14.230
AI is no doubt the next frontier but I don’t
think it’ll replace as many jobs as people

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:15.230
think.

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:19.779
Check out this video to learn more.

